{"ts": 1789783814.273349, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "The sign pattern is well-determined: flipping any sign costs 0.6\u20131.1 Sharpe, far beyond noise. This isn't a knife-edge fit.\n\nNow let me reason about what to expect out-of-sample before submitting.\n\n**Selection bias assessment.** I ran ~70 evaluations. The standard error on an in-sample Sharpe with n=5000 daily observations is roughly \u221a(1/n) annualized \u2248 1/\u221a20yr \u2248 0.22. Picking the max over ~70 correlated draws inflates the estimate by perhaps 1.5\u20132 SE of the *effective* independent search, but my specs are highly overlapping (most share feature 6), so effective search is much smaller than 70 \u2014 maybe 10\u201315 independent looks. That suggests roughly 0.15\u20130.30 of upward selection bias.\n\n**Mitigating evidence that this is real signal, not noise mining:**\n- Feature 6 alone is 0.975, found on the *first* untargeted sweep with no selection at all. That's an unbiased look, and it's the single largest magnitude of 40.\n- Features 7 and 10 were also top-5 on that same unbiased sweep. The winning triple is the combination of three independently-strong, pre-identified features \u2014 not an odd trio surfaced by mining interactions.\n- The gain from 0.975 \u2192 1.256 is modest and consistent with genuine diversification of three imperfectly-correlated signals (pairwise combos of 7/8/10 without 6 run 0.70\u20130.84, so they carry independent information rather than being duplicates of 6).\n\nSo the bulk of the performance rests on a pre-selection finding. I'd discount the 1.256 primarily for the incremental triple-selection step, not for the whole thing.\n\n**Point belief:** shrink 1.256 toward the unbiased anchor. I center at **1.00** \u2014 below feature 6's standalone 0.975 plus a small genuine diversification credit, with the selection premium removed. **SD 0.30**, reflecting genuine estimation error (~0.22) plus regime-shift risk that any of these features decays out-of-sample."}
